This is a basic io_uring prototype.

Goals:

  Throughput performance gains
  Latency gains
  Reduced memory footprint via oversubscribed "provided" buffers
  Aggressive use of MSG_MORE and IORING_CQE_F_SOCK_NONEMPTY

Longer term goals

  QOS possibility with assigned priorities to different work items
  Drive control plane Proton proactor and Core from io_uring loop.
  Back-pressure partially guided by local router's IO and CPU load.

Currently only does TCP to TCP on a single router.  No inter-router support yet.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

Benchmarks:

For latency:
  h2load --warm-up-time 1 --duration 5 --clients 2 --threads 2 http://127.0.0.1:22001/index.txt
  nghttpd 22002 --address 127.0.0.1 --no-tls --htdocs /r4/amqp/s/conf/http2-server/web/ --workers 2

For throughput:
  iperf3 --client 127.0.0.1 --port 20001 --parallel 2
  iperf3 --server --bind 127.0.0.1 --port 20002

The router:
  skrouterd -c rnew2l.conf

The following numbers are reported for 3 runs
  iperf3 separate;
                   h2load separate 
  iperf3 and h2load run concurrently

No router (memory fabric speed limited)
  7.3 GB/s ;  74017.40 req/s, 9.11MB/s, 24us
  7.1 GB/s ;  66465.00 req/s, 8.18MB/s, 27us

Existing router (2 worker threads)
  1.8 GB/s ;  29952.80 req/s, 3.68MB/s, 63us
  1.6 GB/s ;  11849.80 req/s, 1.46MB/s, 165us

This prototype (fixed size 4k buffers, 2 worker threads, 2 rings)
  2.1 GB/s ;  42260.60 req/s, 5.20MB/s, 44us
  1.7 GB/s ;  36208.60 req/s, 4.45MB/s, 52us

This prototype (mixed buffers 2K & 16k, 2 worker threads, 2 rings)
  3.6 GB/s ;  41599.20 req/s, 5.12MB/s, 45us
  2.8 GB/s ;  28173.00 req/s, 3.47MB/s, 68us

------------------------------------------------------------------------

Benchmarks performed on Ryzen 5950X 16 core 32 thread machine.

All TCP connections via loopback.
All TCP connections between clients, servers, and router cross same slow inter-chiplet memory boundary.
Processes of same pinned to specific CPUs to guarantee the chiplet crossing.

The benchmark runs are ridiculously short, but the prototype is still
immature and fragile.  A few longer runs indicate that the numbers are
reasonable ballpark estimates.

